ays of  culture in this chamber  and it's very well known that you can  get cardiomyocytes to beat in culture in  the traditional  2g system and what you see in blue  on the middle left is these  cardiomyocytes beat in  both directions kind of there's not a  directionality of beating  but our colleagues from berkeley have  published on this model they've shown  that  if you pack them into this particular  chamber  you force them to beat only in one  direction so that you can actually  measure contractility i'll be we didn't  do this in our experiments  and at the bottom you can see that we  can establish the same directionality of  beating if you see collar  that means that the cells are pulling in  one direction and they're not pulling  in the orthogonal direction and then you  can test  different drugs and concentration  response you can do  kind of escalation of the dose in the  same device or you can  do other time series or other types of  experiments and you can derive  effective concentrations and you can  compare those to the c-max or  or other types of experiments and again  you can see that  both us and the developers have  published uh  the developers have published a couple  of papers where they've compared  their results to those of human cmax a  couple of  known drugs and these were very similar  which gives confidence in this  technology  that we have not published our data yet  but we get very similar data which again  uh you know is is a good result and  something that we're very glad  to see another model that is based on  uh gravity-driven profusion albeit not  with the  pipette tips but more of the reservoirs  and that particular platform is the  vascularized tumor model from your  california irvine  labs and this particular model  if you see on the right their first  generation was  you know a glass slide with  actual chambers that were filled with  media they have since miniaturized this  into more of a 96  uh well uh plate like um  organizatio